# Each word tells the reader that a step is easy or a behavior is small
# (`simply`, `merely`, `easily`, `straightforward`, `just run`), which
# the reader judges for themselves. Technical writers call these
# minimizing words, because each makes a step sound smaller. None needs
# a guard for a product name or a mention of the word. Both are rare in
# technical prose, and the skill wants the words gone from a sentence
# about behavior. `simply` and `merely` often mean `only` (`requests
# that merely read objects`), so the message offers `only` where the
# word limits the claim.
#
# The rule hit 127 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 16 of 20 sampled
# hits told the reader that a step was easy or a behavior small. The
# misses were `easily` in a warning of a risk (`can easily cause
# errors`), license text, and `quickly` where speed was the point.
#
# `simply` skips a following ` put`, because the restatement rule
# reports `Simply put`. The guard sits glued to the word so it reads
# the token right after it. `Put simply` and `To put it simply` count
# here instead, because no pattern can look back from `simply` to the
# `put` before it.
#
# After a negation the word carries a contrast (`it is not simply an
# offset`), and deleting it flips the claim, so the not-only rule
# applies instead. After `more`, `less`, `most`, or `least` it states a
# real comparison (`reads finish more easily when the cache is warm`).
# Weir has no lookbehind, so the first branch consumes the token before
# the word and checks it against the negations and those four words. The
# filter then narrows the finding back to the word. The comparison guard
# dropped 13 hits in the measure.py corpus, and 13 of 13 compared two
# things.
#
# A space in a pattern is itself a whitespace token, so the first branch
# reads a word, a space, and the minimizer. It needs a token before the
# word, so the other two branches catch a sentence that opens with
# `simply` or `merely`. They need a verb or a glued comma after it
# (`Simply run make`, `Simply, the cache is a map`). Those two also fire
# after a negation when a verb follows (`is not simply copied`), a case
# rare enough to leave.
#
# The other words have no opener branch, so a sentence that opens with
# one passes (`Just run make`). For `just`, such a branch also fires
# after a negation (`you can't just use a container`), where the word
# carries the contrast. `just` counts only before an imperative verb, a
# verb that gives a command (`just run make`). Its other senses, such as
# `only` and `a moment ago` (`just one entry`, `the file was just
# created`), belong to the Optional `just` rule, which offers a fix for
# each sense. `is easy to` and `it's easy to` do not need a guard,
# because a negation splits them (`is not easy to`).
#
# `quickly` counts only after `to`, `you`, or `you can`, and in `quickly
# and easily` (`to quickly set up the project`), where it tells the
# reader that a step is fast. Elsewhere it mostly describes how fast the
# code runs (`the cache fills quickly`). These branches hit 11 lines in
# the measure.py corpus, and 9 of 11 were minimizers. The misses made
# speed the point (`to quickly pinpoint where code is`).
expr neg [not, never, isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't, can't, cannot, couldn't, won't, doesn't, don't, didn't, shouldn't]
expr verbs [run, use, add, call, set, pass, type, install, copy, edit, change, create, delete, remove, update, point, drop, put, write, open, click]
expr word [(simply!(( )put)), merely, easily, trivially, straightforward, (easy to use), (just @verbs)]
expr main [<(![@neg, more, less, most, least] @word), @word>, (simply[(( )<VERB, !put>), (,)]), (merely[(( )VERB), (,)]), (is easy to), (it's easy to), (to quickly), (you quickly), (you can quickly), (quickly and easily)]
let message "Minimizer. Delete the word, or write `only` where it limits the claim."
let description "Flags `simply`, `merely`, `easily`, and similar words that tell the reader a step is easy or a behavior is small."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Flush writes the buffer to disk."
allows "Simply put, the cache is a map."
allows "The offset is not simply an index into the token list."
allows "It isn't merely a cache."
allows "WriteValue cannot easily tell whether the input aliases the buffer."
allows "The migration is not straightforward."
allows "You can't just use a container for this."
allows "The file was just created."
allows "The cache fills quickly under load."
allows "Reads finish more easily when the cache is warm."
